<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuttul</h1><p class="st-meta">2500 BCE – 1760 BCE (approx.) · capital Tuttul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tuttul, a city on the Euphrates near modern Raqqa, sacred to the god Dagan, where Sargon of Akkad prayed before conquering the upper Euphrates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tuttul stood at Tell Bi'a where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balikh-river">Balikh</a> river meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, an ideal place for river and caravan traffic. It was a substantial city by the mid-3rd millennium BCE and became famous as the cult centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, the chief god of the middle Euphrates.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> recorded that he bowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> at Tuttul, who granted him <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ebla">Ebla</a> and the cedar forests. In the 18th century BCE Tuttul was a seat of Amorite tribal kings who fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim">Yahdun-Lim</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and later fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>. German excavations from 1980 uncovered palaces and tombs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, Tuttul declined and lost its independence around 1760 BCE. It remained a minor settlement, and its sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> faded from the record.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 2500 BCE – 1760 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:9.95%;width:14.73%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2500 BCE – 1760 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tuttul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bahlu-kulim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Syrian Semitic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Amorite, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tell Bi'a</dd></dl></aside></div>

